// UCA Viva canonical question bank — SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH.
// Consumed by /quiz/mock-defence.html (flashcards) and /quiz/viva-canonical.js (site viva mode).
// Edit model answers (a) and tactics (warn) HERE ONLY — both consumers read from this file.
// Schema per question: {id, cat, weight, q, a, warn}. Canonical (HRL-correct) figures.
window.VIVA_BANK = [
  {"id":1,"cat":"Contribution & framing","weight":3,"q":"In two sentences, what is the contribution of this thesis?","a":"It tests classic cyber-aggression predictors alongside novel AI-related constructs and personality in a single hierarchical model predicting hostile response likelihood, and shows moral disengagement dominates while the AI block adds no incremental variance. The secondary contribution is the MIRM mediation-dissociation finding: an AI-venting signal that predicts lower hostility independently of the cognitive-script pathway.","warn":"Lead with the empirical finding, not MIRM. MIRM is secondary and contested."},
  {"id":2,"cat":"Contribution & framing","weight":2,"q":"Why does this study matter beyond the existing cyber-aggression literature?","a":"It extends moral disengagement from adolescent samples into a higher-education population and tests whether AI-interaction constructs add anything over established predictors. The null AI result is itself informative: it tells the field the first-generation AI instruments do not yet capture variance in hostility.","warn":""},
  {"id":3,"cat":"Contribution & framing","weight":2,"q":"Who is the intended audience for this work and what should they take from it?","a":"Cyberpsychology researchers and instrument developers. The takeaway is that moral disengagement is the cognitive gateway and that AI constructs need measurement work before they can earn a place in predictive models.","warn":""},
  {"id":4,"cat":"Contribution & framing","weight":1,"q":"If you had to defend this thesis on a single finding, which would it be and why?","a":"Moral disengagement as the dominant predictor (B = 1.59, p < .001; standardised β = .47 (.45 in the structural model)), because it is robust, theoretically grounded in Bandura, and survives entry of competing AI and personality blocks.","warn":""},
  {"id":5,"cat":"Contribution & framing","weight":1,"q":"What is the single biggest weakness of the thesis, in your own assessment?","a":"The AI-construct instrumentation. The block was null and I argue this is measurement failure rather than absence of effect, but cross-sectionally I cannot fully separate those two explanations. That is why the experimental follow-up exists.","warn":"Volunteering this first is strength. Do not get cornered into it."},
  {"id":6,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":3,"q":"Your anonymity coefficient is negative. You predicted positive. Explain.","a":"Perceived anonymity predicted lower hostile response likelihood (B = -0.84, p = .002 in the classic block; standardised β = -.21, p = .015 in the full hierarchical model). This contradicts Suler's online disinhibition account. I keep H1 as the theory-derived directional prediction and treat the reversal as a substantive finding requiring explanation, not as a failed hypothesis to be hidden.","warn":"State the number and the p-value without prompting. Do not hedge on the direction."},
  {"id":7,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":3,"q":"Is the negative anonymity effect real, or a measurement artefact?","a":"I cannot rule out measurement entirely, but the effect is significant (p = .015) and survives the full classic block. The Pan et al. (2023) finding that anonymity can enhance moral courage gives a theoretical route: anonymity may reduce reputational stakes that otherwise license retaliation. I present it as anomalous and theoretically open, not resolved.","warn":"Do not claim a mechanism you did not test. 'Consistent with', not 'because of'."},
  {"id":8,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":2,"q":"What does 'theory lags infrastructure' mean and why is it in Chapter 2 rather than Chapter 5?","a":"Disinhibition theory was formed on an earlier internet architecture; today's identified, platform-mediated environments may invert anonymity's effect. I place the argument in Chapter 2 so the anomaly is anticipated by the framing rather than rescued post hoc in the discussion.","warn":"This is the protective placement. Be ready to justify it as pre-emptive, not opportunistic."},
  {"id":9,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":2,"q":"How do you reconcile your anonymity finding with the SIDE model?","a":"SIDE predicts anonymity amplifies salient group norms rather than uniformly increasing aggression. If the salient norm in a university sample is non-hostile, SIDE actually predicts anonymity could lower hostility, which is broadly consistent with my negative coefficient.","warn":""},
  {"id":10,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":1,"q":"Could the anonymity item simply be measuring something other than anonymity?","a":"Possibly. It may tap perceived consequence-freedom rather than identity concealment. I flag this as a construct-validity limitation and a target for the multi-item replacement in future work.","warn":""},
  {"id":11,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":1,"q":"If you re-ran this and anonymity came out positive, what would you conclude?","a":"That the present sample or instrument produced an unstable estimate, and I would weight the meta-analytic Suler-consistent direction. A single anomalous coefficient is a prompt for replication, not a refutation of disinhibition theory.","warn":""},
  {"id":12,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":1,"q":"Why not simply drop the anonymity hypothesis given the reversal?","a":"Dropping it would be results-driven hypothesising. The honest record is that theory predicted positive, data showed negative, and the discrepancy is the interesting result.","warn":""},
  {"id":13,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":3,"q":"Your outcome changed from the pre-registered CAI scale to a single-item HRL. Justify this.","a":"The change happened during instrument development, before any data collection. No CAI data were ever collected, so the outcome cannot have been chosen to fit results. HRL operationalises Grigg's cyber-aggression construct through a scenario-based route consistent with Tennakoon et al.'s closing recommendation.","warn":"Say 'before data collection' and 'no CAI data ever collected' explicitly. This is the whole defence."},
  {"id":14,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":3,"q":"A single-item dependent variable is psychometrically weak. Defend it.","a":"Single-item measures are defensible for concrete, narrow constructs and reduce respondent fatigue in a vignette design. HRL is anchored to a specific scenario, which constrains interpretation. I acknowledge the reliability limitation and treat it as a bound on, not a refutation of, the findings.","warn":"Concede the limitation. Do not pretend a single item is as good as a scale."},
  {"id":14.5,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":1,"q":"How would you estimate reliability for a single-item outcome?","a":"Classical alpha is undefined for one item. I rely on the construct's narrowness and scenario-anchoring for content validity, and I note test-retest would be the appropriate reliability check in future work.","warn":""},
  {"id":15,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":2,"q":"Walk me through the timeline that proves the switch was not results-driven.","a":"Instrument development and the CAI-to-HRL decision are documented before data collection opened. The first valid responses postdate the decision. The documentary trail is in the methodology and the AI-use log.","warn":"Have the dates ready. Vagueness here is fatal."},
  {"id":16,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":2,"q":"Does the Grigg construct survive the move to HRL?","a":"Yes. Grigg (2010) defines cyber-aggression conceptually; HRL is one operationalisation of that definition via scenario-based response likelihood. The construct is preserved; only the measurement route changed.","warn":""},
  {"id":17,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":1,"q":"Why is HRL scored 1 to 10 rather than on the 1 to 5 Likert scale used elsewhere?","a":"A wider response range gives the outcome more discriminating power as the sole DV, while predictor scales stay on their validated 1 to 5 metric. The mixed scaling is handled by standardisation in the regression.","warn":""},
  {"id":18,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":1,"q":"What did you lose by abandoning CAI?","a":"Multi-item reliability and direct comparability to studies using CAI. What I gained was a cleaner scenario-anchored outcome and freedom from a scale that was underperforming in development.","warn":""},
  {"id":19,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":3,"q":"The entire AI construct block was non-significant. Why is that not just a failed hypothesis?","a":"The AI predictors entered stepwise and each added negligible variance: AI trust ΔR² = .002 (p = .59), AI disinhibition ΔR² = .020 (F(1,131) = 3.45, p = .066), AI familiarity ΔR² = .001 (p = .62). I frame this as measurement validity failure because the constructs were measured with first-generation, partly self-authored items lacking established psychometric pedigree. A null from weak instruments is uninformative about the underlying theory.","warn":"You must be able to distinguish 'measurement failed' from 'theory wrong'. Have the discriminant evidence ready (next Q)."},
  {"id":20,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":3,"q":"How do you know the AI null is measurement failure and not simply that AI constructs do not predict hostility?","a":"Two lines. First, the AD5 venting item produced a significant zero-order signal (r = -.203, p = .0155) and survived full cognitive-script controls (B = -0.374, p = .030), so at least one AI item carries real variance. Second, the AI increments carried negligible effect sizes — AI disinhibition, the largest, had f² = .026 versus f² = .282 for the classic block — so at n = 139 the design could not reliably detect them; the null is as consistent with under-detection as with true absence.","warn":"This is the crux question. If you cannot answer it cleanly the null reads as theory failure."},
  {"id":21,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":2,"q":"What was statistical power for detecting the AI block effect?","a":"The thesis reports effect sizes rather than post-hoc power. The AI increments were negligible — AI disinhibition, the largest, had f² = .026 — against f² = .282 for the classic block (Cohen: .02 small, .15 medium, .35 large). The asymmetry means the design was adequate for the classic predictors and far too small to detect the AI effects.","warn":"Know both power figures. The contrast is your friend."},
  {"id":22,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":2,"q":"If the AI block is null, why keep it in the thesis at all?","a":"Because a documented, transparently reported null on novel constructs is a contribution: it tells the field the instruments need development before the constructs can be tested fairly. Suppressing it would be a worse scientific practice.","warn":""},
  {"id":23,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":1,"q":"Which AI construct, if any, showed the most promise?","a":"AI disinhibition, specifically the AD5 venting item, which carried the one AI-side signal and anchors the MIRM mediation-dissociation analysis. But it is a trace, not a robust effect: significant within the classic predictor set (B = -0.37, β = -.17, p = .030) yet falling to non-significance in the saturated composite model (β = -.18, p = .16). I present it as a signal worth pursuing, not a confirmed finding.","warn":"Concede the fragility unprompted: significant in the classic set, n.s. in the saturated model. Do not call it 'reliable'."},
  {"id":24,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":1,"q":"Could the AI null be a sample issue rather than a measurement issue?","a":"A student sample with relatively homogeneous AI exposure could restrict range and depress the effect. That is a sampling contributor to the same conclusion: the cross-sectional design cannot adjudicate, so the experimental study is required.","warn":""},
  {"id":25,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":1,"q":"Reviewers often distrust 'measurement failure' as an excuse for a null. Why should I believe you?","a":"Because I commit to it as a testable claim, not a shield: the follow-up study replaces the items with psychometrically developed scales, and if the effect stays null under good measurement, the theory is wrong. Flake and Fried (2020) is the standard I am holding myself to.","warn":""},
  {"id":26,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":3,"q":"Does MIRM improve on the additive GAM model?","a":"No, not on fit. Canonical layered comparison: GAM R² = .209 with the best AIC (663.2). The I-cubed interaction layer added nothing (ΔR² = .022, p = .43). The MIRM regulatory layer was borderline (ΔR² = .033, p = .0554) and lost to the parsimonious GAM on AIC. MIRM is not justified as an incremental-variance improvement.","warn":"Say 'no' first. The moment you imply MIRM beats GAM on fit, an external who knows model comparison ends you."},
  {"id":27,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":3,"q":"Then what is MIRM actually for, if it does not beat GAM?","a":"Its genuine independent contribution is the mediation dissociation. AI venting predicts lower hostility net of the full cognitive-script pathway (MD, normalisation, empathy, anonymity): B = -0.374, standardised beta = -0.167, p = .030. MD lives among cognitive-script constructs; venting lives with neuroticism. They occupy structurally different neighbourhoods, which is a pattern neither pure GAM nor pure I-cubed predicts. This is the classic-predictor-set result; the venting effect is a trace, not robust — it falls to non-significance in the saturated composite model (β = -.18, p = .16), so I offer the dissociation as a signal worth pursuing, not a confirmed mechanism.","warn":"'Consistent with regulation as a distinct mechanism', never 'proves'. Cross-sectional cannot confirm."},
  {"id":28,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":2,"q":"Your boundary-condition test only works under a median split. Is that not a red flag?","a":"Yes, and I disclose it. The continuous venting-by-impellance interaction was non-significant (β = -.31, p = .18). The effect appears only via median split: high-impellance slope β = -1.21, p = .005; low-impellance slope β = -0.25, ns; the difference was significant by bootstrap (Δ = -0.97, 95% CI [-1.80, -0.09], p = .032). Double fragility: split-only, and the impellor (neuroticism) is empirically weak against HRL. I weight this to the experimental study.","warn":"Disclose BOTH fragilities unprompted. This is the most overclaim-prone result in the thesis."},
  {"id":29,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":2,"q":"Why propose a new framework (MIRM) when I-cubed already exists and your data do not support the multiplicative core?","a":"MIRM is positioned as a cybercommunications regulatory extension, not a replacement, and I am explicit that the cross-sectional data support only the regulatory dissociation leg, not the multiplicative interaction core. The framework is a structured prediction set for the three-study programme, not a claim of confirmed superiority.","warn":"Demote, don't drop. Frame MIRM as a hypothesis-generator, not a validated model."},
  {"id":30,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":2,"q":"What is the difference between MIRM and Finkel's I-cubed?","a":"I-cubed organises aggression into instigation, impellance, and inhibition with a multiplicative core. MIRM adds a regulatory substrate specific to computer-mediated channels, hypothesising that AI mediation can supply external regulation that modulates the inhibition term. The dissertation tests MIRM; the inter-personal companion idea (Babel Fish Theory) is a separate paper.","warn":"Keep BFT out of the viva unless asked. It is not in the thesis."},
  {"id":31,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":1,"q":"Earlier drafts cited beta = -.452 for the residual venting effect. Why does your thesis say -0.374?","a":"The -.452 was a loose-run figure with a raw-versus-standardised mislabel. The canonical value is raw B = -0.374, standardised beta = -0.167, p = .0303. Only the canonical figure appears in the thesis.","warn":"If this surfaces, owning the correction shows rigour. Never quote -.452."},
  {"id":32,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":1,"q":"Can a cross-sectional design ever support a mediation claim like MIRM's?","a":"It can show patterns consistent with mediation but cannot establish causal direction. I state plainly that definitive tests require the experimental and experience-sampling studies. The cross-sectional result is a consistency check, not a confirmation.","warn":""},
  {"id":33,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":1,"q":"Why report MIRM at all if it is this fragile?","a":"Because the dissociation leg is genuinely independent and significant, and because transparent reporting of a borderline framework is more defensible than either suppressing it or overselling it. The fragility is disclosed, not hidden.","warn":""},
  {"id":34,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":1,"q":"What AIC difference would you have needed to claim MIRM superiority?","a":"Conventionally a difference of at least 2 to 4 in MIRM's favour. Instead GAM had the lowest AIC, so the parsimony criterion favours the simpler model and I report that against my own framework.","warn":""},
  {"id":35,"cat":"Moral disengagement","weight":3,"q":"Why does moral disengagement dominate every model?","a":"Standardised β = .47 in the full model (.45 in the structural model), B = 1.59, p < .001. Theoretically it may be the cognitive gateway through which other risk factors operate (Bandura): if anonymity, empathy deficit, and normalisation influence hostility partly through moral disengagement, MD should dominate any model containing both cognitive and environmental variables, which is what the data show.","warn":""},
  {"id":36,"cat":"Moral disengagement","weight":2,"q":"Which Bandura mechanisms of moral disengagement are most relevant to your items?","a":"Moral justification, euphemistic labelling, displacement of responsibility, and attribution of blame map most directly onto online retaliation scenarios. Bandura et al. (1996) is the measurement lineage.","warn":""},
  {"id":37,"cat":"Moral disengagement","weight":2,"q":"Tell me about the md2 item problem.","a":"The md2 item ('justified in attacking them back') correlates with HRL at r = .49 versus .14 to .33 for the other MD items. It is effectively a trace of the original CAI outcome embedded in the predictor block. I run a sensitivity refit of Model 5 without md2 and report it as a Section 4.5 footnote so the MD effect is shown not to depend on a contaminated item.","warn":"Raise this yourself. An examiner who spots it unprompted is worse than you disclosing it."},
  {"id":38,"cat":"Moral disengagement","weight":1,"q":"Does the MD effect survive removing md2?","a":"Yes; the sensitivity refit retains a significant MD effect, which is the point of reporting it. MD dominance is not an artefact of the single high-correlating item.","warn":"Confirm against the 00_prep_md5.R output before the viva. Do not state the surviving beta from memory."},
  {"id":39,"cat":"Moral disengagement","weight":1,"q":"Is moral disengagement a cause or a correlate of hostility here?","a":"Cross-sectionally, a correlate. The gateway interpretation is theoretical. I do not claim causation from these data.","warn":""},
  {"id":40,"cat":"Moral disengagement","weight":1,"q":"How does extending MD to a higher-education sample add value, given the adolescent literature?","a":"It demonstrates the construct retains predictive power in adults and in a context with novel AI and personality competitors in the model, which the adolescent work did not test.","warn":""},
  {"id":41,"cat":"Method & design","weight":3,"q":"Why a vignette-based survey rather than a behavioural or experimental design?","a":"Vignettes give standardised, ethically safe exposure to provocation that could not be staged behaviourally without harm. Aguinis and Bradley (2014) set the design standard I follow. The trade-off is that intentions are not behaviour, which I state.","warn":""},
  {"id":42,"cat":"Method & design","weight":2,"q":"How did you guard against the gap between stated intention and actual behaviour?","a":"I cannot close it cross-sectionally. I anchor HRL to a concrete scenario to reduce abstraction, and I treat the outcome as response likelihood, not enacted aggression, throughout the claims.","warn":""},
  {"id":43,"cat":"Method & design","weight":2,"q":"Walk me through your analytic strategy.","a":"Hierarchical regression entering classic predictors, then the AI block, then personality, with HRL as the sole DV. Reliability and descriptives first, then the blocked models, then MIRM exploratory tests (layered comparison, mediation dissociation, boundary condition), then sensitivity refits.","warn":""},
  {"id":44,"cat":"Method & design","weight":2,"q":"Why hierarchical regression rather than SEM as the primary analysis?","a":"Sample size and the single-item outcome make blocked regression the honest primary tool; it answers the incremental-variance question directly. A PLS-SEM sensitivity check is reported, but regression carries the main inference.","warn":""},
  {"id":45,"cat":"Method & design","weight":1,"q":"How were the vignettes constructed and validated?","a":"Scenario-based provocations designed to elicit a hostile-response judgement, developed during instrument construction. Validation was content-based rather than empirical pilot at scale, which I note as a limitation.","warn":"Be honest about the level of vignette piloting actually done. Do not invent a pilot study."},
  {"id":46,"cat":"Method & design","weight":1,"q":"Did the order of vignettes or items introduce bias?","a":"Order effects are a possible confound; if randomisation or counterbalancing was applied, state it; if not, flag it as a limitation. I report what was actually done in the procedure.","warn":"Check the actual procedure before answering. Do not claim counterbalancing you did not implement."},
  {"id":47,"cat":"Method & design","weight":1,"q":"Why single-item-per-trait personality measurement?","a":"Brevity in an already long instrument. The TIPI lineage (Gosling, Rentfrow and Swann, 2003; Woods and Hampson, 2005) supports short Big Five measurement. Only agreeableness showed a meaningful zero-order relationship (r = -.22, p = .005).","warn":"Cite the TIPI defence proactively; examiners always probe single-item Big Five."},
  {"id":48,"cat":"Method & design","weight":1,"q":"What is the role of the attention check in your design?","a":"It screened careless responding. Six respondents failed it, taking the 170 consented responses down to 164 valid cases. The ATU eligibility filter excluded nobody; all 170 consenting respondents were ATU students.","warn":"Know the 173 → 170 → 164 path (raw, consent, attention); the final drop was attention-check only."},
  {"id":49,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":3,"q":"What is your sample size, and which N applies to which analysis?","a":"173 raw, 170 after the consent gate, 164 valid after the attention check. N = 164 for the DV and descriptives. n = 142 for the AI-block scales because of branching. n = 139 complete cases for the hierarchical regression. Every statistic carries its own N.","warn":"Have these exact: 173 raw, 170 consented, 164 valid, 142 predictor, 139 regression."},
  {"id":50,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":2,"q":"Is n = 139 adequate for a hierarchical regression with this many predictors?","a":"For the classic block, yes: its effect was large (f² = .282). For the tiny AI increments (f² ≤ .026) the design was underpowered. So it is adequate for the effects that matter and transparently underpowered for the null block.","warn":""},
  {"id":51,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":2,"q":"Did you run an a priori power analysis?","a":"Power is reported against the observed effects via G*Power conventions (Faul et al., 2009). State the a priori target if one was set during design; if power is reported post hoc, say so honestly rather than dressing it as a priori.","warn":"Do not claim an a priori power analysis if you only have observed/post-hoc power. Be precise."},
  {"id":52,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":1,"q":"Describe your sample demographics.","a":"164 valid: 65.9% women (108), 29.9% men (49), 2.4% non-binary, 0.6% genderfluid, 1.2% prefer not to say. Demographics sit in Chapter 4, not the participants method section.","warn":"73% female is the STALE wrong figure. The correct figure is 65.9% women."},
  {"id":53,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":1,"q":"How does the gender skew affect your conclusions?","a":"A two-thirds female sample limits generalisation, particularly given gender effects in some cyber-aggression work. I treat it as a generalisability boundary and a target for the replication study.","warn":""},
  {"id":54,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":1,"q":"Why does the AI block have n = 142 while the DV has n = 164?","a":"Branching: only respondents on the relevant survey path answered the AI-block scales. The 164 versus 142 versus 139 chain reflects attention-check loss, then branching, then listwise completeness for the regression.","warn":""},
  {"id":55,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":1,"q":"Was a student convenience sample appropriate for this question?","a":"It suits an instrument-development and first-test study and gives a relatively AI-literate population, but it restricts range and generalisability. The follow-up uses a broader recruited sample.","warn":""},
  {"id":56,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":3,"q":"Report your scale reliabilities.","a":"Aggression normalisation alpha = .67, AI trust alpha = .84, agreeableness on n = 163. Other reported alphas include OD .73, ED .75, anonymity .78, MD .82, AD .84. Composites were built with trust4 reversed and na.rm = FALSE, screened to 164.","warn":"Aggression normalisation .67 and AI trust .84 are the corrected values. Have the full reliability table to hand."},
  {"id":57,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":2,"q":"Aggression normalisation alpha is .67, below the conventional .70. How do you defend using it?","a":"It is marginal, not unacceptable, for a short construct scale, and I report it transparently. I treat findings involving that scale with appropriate caution and note it as a measurement limitation.","warn":"Do not call .67 'good'. Call it marginal and report it."},
  {"id":58,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":2,"q":"What is construct validity and how did you establish it for your novel constructs?","a":"Construct validity (Cronbach and Meehl, 1955) is whether the instrument measures the intended theoretical construct. For the novel AI constructs I established only face and content validity; convergent and discriminant validity were not formally established, which is exactly why the AI block is treated as a measurement question.","warn":""},
  {"id":59,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":2,"q":"How did you handle missing data?","a":"The hierarchical regression uses n = 139 complete cases (listwise). A PLS-SEM sensitivity analysis addresses missingness and imputation separately. Composites used na.rm = FALSE so partial scales did not silently average.","warn":""},
  {"id":60,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":1,"q":"Why na.rm = FALSE rather than mean-imputing within a scale?","a":"To avoid manufacturing composite scores from incomplete item sets, which would understate measurement uncertainty. A respondent missing items on a scale does not get a composite for that scale.","warn":""},
  {"id":61,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":1,"q":"What is the reverse-scored item issue with AI trust?","a":"trust4 was reverse-keyed and recoded before composite construction. Failing to reverse it would have depressed the alpha and distorted the trust composite.","warn":""},
  {"id":62,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":1,"q":"How do you respond to a charge of questionable measurement practices?","a":"I report all alphas, all Ns, the reverse-scoring, the missing-data rule, and the sensitivity refits, which is the transparency Flake and Fried (2020) call for. The novel-construct weakness is named, not buried.","warn":""},
  {"id":63,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":1,"q":"Could common-method variance inflate your correlations?","a":"Yes; all measures are self-report in one sitting, so common-method variance is a plausible inflator. It makes the AI null more striking, not less, since shared method should if anything help the block.","warn":""},
  {"id":64,"cat":"Ethics & AI-use","weight":2,"q":"What ethical approval did this study have?","a":"ATU Research Ethics Committee approval by email dated 12 January 2026. No separate reference number is issued; the approval email itself is the documentary evidence, reproduced in Appendix A.","warn":"There is no REC reference number. The email IS the approval. Do not invent a number."},
  {"id":65,"cat":"Ethics & AI-use","weight":2,"q":"How did you use AI tools in producing this thesis, and how is that documented?","a":"AI was used for citation work, structural auditing, raw material, code, and as a thinking partner. It did not write dissertation prose; I rewrote everything in my own voice. Usage is logged for examiners in a dedicated AI-use log.","warn":"The clean log is a strength. Reference it confidently."},
  {"id":66,"cat":"Ethics & AI-use","weight":2,"q":"Given your topic is AI use, is there a conflict in using AI to produce the thesis?","a":"No, provided it is transparent. I treat my own AI use as documented and bounded, and the distinction between AI as a research-support tool and AI as a measured construct is kept explicit.","warn":""},
  {"id":67,"cat":"Ethics & AI-use","weight":1,"q":"How did you obtain informed consent and protect participants?","a":"Consent preceded the survey; all 170 respondents consented. Data were anonymous self-report with no sensitive identifiers, and the vignette design avoided exposing participants to real aggression.","warn":""},
  {"id":68,"cat":"Ethics & AI-use","weight":1,"q":"Were there risks in asking participants about hostile responses?","a":"Minimal; participants rated likelihood in hypothetical scenarios rather than disclosing real perpetration, which lowers both distress and self-incrimination risk.","warn":""},
  {"id":69,"cat":"Ethics & AI-use","weight":1,"q":"How would an examiner verify your AI-use claims?","a":"Through the AI-use log, which records what was delegated and what was authored. The supervisor constraint was that AI produced no dissertation prose, and the log evidences that.","warn":""},
  {"id":70,"cat":"Theory","weight":2,"q":"How does the General Aggression Model relate to your framework?","a":"GAM provides the additive person-plus-situation baseline that my classic block instantiates. MIRM is positioned as an extension addressing the mediated, regulated nature of computer-mediated channels that GAM does not specify.","warn":""},
  {"id":71,"cat":"Theory","weight":2,"q":"Explain the relevance of CASA (Computers Are Social Actors) to your AI constructs.","a":"CASA (Nass and Moon) predicts people apply social rules to computers, which underwrites treating AI interaction as socially meaningful and therefore as a plausible regulator of affect before a hostile response. It motivates the AI-venting hypothesis.","warn":""},
  {"id":72,"cat":"Theory","weight":2,"q":"How does the extended mind thesis (Clark and Chalmers) feature in your reasoning?","a":"It supports treating AI as a cognitive and potentially affective offload, where part of the regulatory work that would normally be internal is externalised to the AI. This grounds the regulatory-substrate idea in MIRM.","warn":""},
  {"id":73,"cat":"Theory","weight":1,"q":"What is social presence theory contributing here?","a":"Social presence (Biocca, IJsselsteijn) frames how strongly a mediated interlocutor feels present, which is the construct family the post-thesis programme develops into an AI Social Presence Scale.","warn":""},
  {"id":74,"cat":"Theory","weight":1,"q":"How does Suler's online disinhibition effect sit with your overall results?","a":"It motivated the disinhibition and anonymity predictions, but my anonymity coefficient runs against the toxic-disinhibition reading, which is the central theoretical tension the discussion addresses.","warn":""},
  {"id":75,"cat":"Theory","weight":1,"q":"Why does the catharsis / venting hypothesis run opposite to the classic catharsis-is-a-myth literature?","a":"Classic catharsis research concerns venting through aggression itself, which fails. AI venting is venting to a non-target interlocutor, which is closer to regulated disclosure than to aggressive rehearsal, so a protective sign is not inconsistent.","warn":"Do not claim you have proven catharsis. The venting signal is modest and cross-sectional."},
  {"id":76,"cat":"Theory","weight":1,"q":"What is the I-cubed impellance term and how did you operationalise it?","a":"Impellance is the dispositional or situational push toward aggression once instigated. I operationalised it as neuroticism, which proved empirically weak against HRL (r approx -.02), a limitation I disclose in the boundary-condition analysis.","warn":"The impellor being empirically dead is part of the honest disclosure. Do not hide it."},
  {"id":77,"cat":"Theory","weight":1,"q":"How does SIDE differ from classic deindividuation theory?","a":"Classic deindividuation says anonymity erodes self-regulation and increases anti-normative behaviour. SIDE (Reicher, Spears, Postmes) says anonymity shifts identity from personal to social and amplifies whatever group norm is salient, which can be pro- or anti-social.","warn":""},
  {"id":78,"cat":"Theory","weight":1,"q":"Why is Babel Fish Theory not in the dissertation?","a":"Because BFT is the standalone theoretical layer of the wider programme, not the empirical dissertation. It is an inter-personal account in which AI mediates between human nervous systems whose biological co-regulation has been degraded by high-entropy digital channels — a new node type that restores inter-personal translation, letting a user decode the other party's signal as something other than threat. The dissertation tests MIRM, the cross-sectional empirical layer; MIRM and BFT share the regulatory-prosthetic / new-node-type claim but are distinct objects and must not be conflated. BFT (formerly VCM) is reserved for a standalone companion paper in New Media & Society, post-examination.","warn":"Only raise BFT if the examiner does — keep it outside the thesis. If pressed, name the journal (New Media & Society) and do not conflate MIRM (empirical layer) with BFT (theoretical companion)."},
  {"id":79,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":3,"q":"What are the three most important limitations of this study?","a":"First, cross-sectional design cannot establish causal direction. Second, the single-item HRL outcome and the under-developed AI constructs limit measurement strength. Third, the student, female-skewed convenience sample limits generalisability and range.","warn":"Have a crisp three. Rambling here looks like you have not thought about it."},
  {"id":80,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":2,"q":"What would you do differently if you started again?","a":"Develop and validate the AI constructs as multi-item scales before the main study, and use a multi-item or behavioural outcome instead of a single HRL item. That directly addresses the two measurement limitations.","warn":""},
  {"id":81,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":2,"q":"Describe your planned three-study programme.","a":"An escalating design: a cross-sectional survey extension adding three AI construct families, then a three-condition experimental 'Threshold Test', then an experience-sampling study. Each tightens causal inference the cross-sectional thesis cannot reach.","warn":""},
  {"id":82,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":2,"q":"How does the experimental study resolve the AI-block null?","a":"By replacing weak items with developed scales and manipulating AI interaction rather than measuring it, so a persistent null would then implicate the theory rather than the instruments.","warn":""},
  {"id":83,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":1,"q":"What are the three new construct families in the post-thesis programme?","a":"An AI Social Presence Scale, an AI Interaction Function Scale (emotional/venting and instrumental subscales, with a third subscale still to be finalised), and a Perceived AI Quasi-Agency Scale.","warn":"The third AI Interaction Function subscale is not yet recovered. Say 'to be finalised', do not invent it."},
  {"id":84,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":1,"q":"What is the 'Threshold Test' experiment?","a":"A three-condition experimental design probing whether AI interaction shifts the threshold at which provocation produces a hostile response. It is the causal test of the MIRM regulatory claim.","warn":""},
  {"id":85,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":1,"q":"How generalisable are your findings beyond Irish higher education?","a":"Cautiously. The MD dominance is consistent with a broad literature and likely travels; the anonymity reversal and AI-venting signal need replication before any generalisation.","warn":""},
  {"id":86,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":1,"q":"What would falsify your MIRM regulatory claim?","a":"A well-measured, well-powered study showing no AI-venting effect on hostility once cognitive-script predictors are controlled, and no experimental shift in the provocation threshold. I have specified those tests.","warn":""},
  {"id":87,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":1,"q":"Is experience-sampling feasible for this population, and what does it add?","a":"It adds within-person, near-real-time coupling of AI interaction and hostility, addressing the intention-versus-behaviour and direction-of-effect gaps. Feasibility depends on compliance, which is the main risk.","warn":""},
  {"id":88,"cat":"Method & design","weight":1,"q":"How did you decide the order of blocks in the hierarchical regression?","a":"Classic, established predictors first to set the baseline, then the novel AI block to test incremental value, then personality. The order reflects the theoretical question: does AI add anything over what we already know.","warn":""},
  {"id":89,"cat":"Measurement & validity","weight":1,"q":"Did you check regression assumptions (normality, multicollinearity, homoscedasticity)?","a":"State what was actually checked: VIFs for multicollinearity, residual plots for normality and homoscedasticity. Report any breaches and how they were handled.","warn":"Confirm against the actual diagnostics output before the viva. Do not claim clean assumptions blind."},
  {"id":90,"cat":"Moral disengagement","weight":1,"q":"Could the MD-HRL relationship be tautological, given both concern willingness to harm?","a":"It is a fair concern, which is exactly why the md2 sensitivity analysis matters: removing the most outcome-proximal item tests whether MD predicts beyond near-tautology, and the effect survives.","warn":""},
  {"id":91,"cat":"Contribution & framing","weight":1,"q":"Summarise your results for a non-specialist in 30 seconds.","a":"How willing people are to hit back online is driven mostly by whether they can morally excuse it, not by anonymity or by how they use AI. Venting to an AI may slightly reduce hostility, but the AI measures here were too rough to trust, so that needs proper testing.","warn":""},
  {"id":92,"cat":"AI-block null result","weight":1,"q":"What does ai_freq show in your models?","a":"AI-use frequency is not a significant predictor in the hierarchical or path models; it enters mainly as a control in the venting partial correlation. The one AI-frequency signal is age-stratified: within the 18-24 cohort, daily AI users reported lower HRL than less frequent users (t = -2.47, p = .016, d = 0.45), convergent with the venting trace. It does not rescue the AI block's null incremental variance.","warn":"Report this as isolated, not as evidence the AI block works."},
  {"id":93,"cat":"MIRM / model comparison","weight":1,"q":"Why is the mediation dissociation more credible than the boundary condition?","a":"Because it holds on the full sample with continuous controls and a clean residual test (p = .030), whereas the boundary condition holds only under a median split with a weak impellor. One is robust-ish, the other fragile, and I rank them that way.","warn":""},
  {"id":94,"cat":"Anonymity anomaly","weight":1,"q":"Does your anonymity finding undermine the rest of the thesis?","a":"No. It is one coefficient against theory in a model where the dominant, theory-consistent effect is moral disengagement. It sharpens the discussion rather than destabilising the core finding.","warn":""},
  {"id":95,"cat":"CAI to HRL switch","weight":1,"q":"Is HRL comparable to outcomes used in the studies you cite?","a":"Partially. It shares the response-likelihood logic of scenario-based cyber-aggression measures such as Tennakoon's, but differs in being single-item, which limits direct effect-size comparison.","warn":""},
  {"id":96,"cat":"Sample & power","weight":1,"q":"How does attrition from 142 to 139 affect the regression?","a":"Three cases lost to listwise deletion on the regression variables. The loss is small and unlikely to bias estimates materially, but I report the complete-case N rather than masking it with imputation in the primary model.","warn":""},
  {"id":97,"cat":"Theory","weight":1,"q":"How does Walther's hyperpersonal model relate to your AI-mediation argument?","a":"Hyperpersonal CMC shows mediated channels can intensify rather than dilute interpersonal process. By analogy, AI mediation could intensify regulatory as well as aggressive dynamics, which motivates studying its function rather than assuming a direction.","warn":""},
  {"id":98,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":1,"q":"If the AI constructs keep failing to predict, is the whole AI-cyber-aggression research line worth pursuing?","a":"Yes, but as a measurement-first programme. The venting signal and the ai_freq effect suggest something is there; the responsible path is to build valid instruments before declaring the constructs predictively inert.","warn":""},
  {"id":99,"cat":"Contribution & framing","weight":1,"q":"What is the one sentence you want the examiners to remember?","a":"Moral disengagement, not anonymity or AI use, is the engine of online hostility in this sample, and the AI question remains open because the measures, not the theory, were the limiting factor.","warn":""},
  {"id":100,"cat":"Limitations & future work","weight":1,"q":"Where do you want this research to be in five years?","a":"A validated AI-interaction-function instrument and an experimental and experience-sampling evidence base that settles whether AI mediation genuinely modulates the provocation-to-hostility pathway, turning MIRM from a borderline cross-sectional pattern into a tested mechanism.","warn":""},
  // ===== Round 2 (added 2026-06-12): statistical literacy probes, hostile curveballs, viva process & opening =====
  {"id":101,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":2,"q":"Your tables report both B and β. Explain the difference and when each matters.","a":"B is unstandardised: units of outcome per unit of predictor — MD's B = 1.59 means roughly 1.6 points of HRL on the 1-to-10 scale per scale-point of MD. β is standardised: SD change in HRL per SD of predictor — MD's β = .47. B is for interpretation in raw units; β is for ranking predictors against each other within the model.","warn":"Use the MD pair (B = 1.59, β = .47) as the worked example — your own numbers beat textbook ones."},
  {"id":102,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":2,"q":"Explain ΔR² to me as if I were a first-year.","a":"R² is the share of variance in the outcome the model explains. ΔR² is how much that share rises when a new block enters. The additive model explained about 21% of HRL variance (R² = .209); adding AI trust raised that by .002 — two tenths of one percent. ΔR² measures incremental explanatory value, not total.","warn":"This is a pure competence probe. Answer it in plain language without notes."},
  {"id":103,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":1,"q":"Why AIC for model comparison rather than BIC?","a":"AIC targets predictive adequacy; BIC penalises parameters more harshly at this n and would punish MIRM's extra terms even more. The verdict is insensitive to the choice: GAM had the lowest AIC (663.2), and a harsher criterion only strengthens parsimony's win. I report AIC and note the conclusion does not change under either criterion.","warn":"The key sentence: the verdict does not change under either criterion."},
  {"id":104,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":2,"q":"Your boundary-condition test rests on a bootstrap CI. What does that interval actually mean?","a":"Resample the data with replacement, recompute the slope difference each time, and take the central 95% of the resulting distribution: Δ = -0.97, 95% CI [-1.80, -0.09]. The interval excludes zero, so the split-sample slope difference is significant (p = .032). Bootstrapping is distribution-free, which suits a median-split-derived quantity — but it cannot repair the split-only fragility, which I disclose alongside it.","warn":"Tie it straight back to the disclosed fragility — the CI is sound arithmetic on a fragile design."},
  {"id":105,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":2,"q":"Define f² and compute it for me.","a":"Cohen's f² = ΔR² / (1 − R²_full): a block's incremental variance scaled against the variance left unexplained by the full model. The classic block carried f² = .282, between Cohen's medium (.15) and large (.35) benchmarks; AI disinhibition, the largest AI increment, carried f² = .026, barely above the small threshold of .02. That asymmetry is the power argument in one line.","warn":"Be ready to write the formula on paper. Externals use this exact question as a competence probe."},
  {"id":106,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":2,"q":"The MIRM regulatory layer came in at p = .0554. Marginally significant, surely?","a":"No. It is non-significant at α = .05 and I report it as such. 'Marginal significance' is a rhetorical category, not a statistical one. The layered-comparison verdict stands on ΔR² = .033, p = .0554, and the AIC loss to GAM. Treating .0554 as quasi-confirmation would be exactly the overclaim the thesis is built to avoid.","warn":"The examiner may be offering you rope. Refuse it crisply — against your own framework."},
  {"id":107,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":1,"q":"What does a VIF tell you, and what threshold did you apply?","a":"VIF quantifies how much a coefficient's variance is inflated by collinearity with the other predictors: 1/(1 − Rj²), with conventional alarms at 5 to 10. State the actual values from the diagnostics output; do not quote a threshold you have not checked your own VIFs against.","warn":"Same rule as the assumptions question: confirm the real VIFs from the output before the viva."},
  {"id":108,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":1,"q":"Listwise deletion assumes what about the missingness mechanism?","a":"MCAR — missing completely at random. Under MAR or MNAR, complete-case estimates can be biased. Here the exposure is three cases (142 to 139), and the PLS-SEM sensitivity run handles missingness differently and reaches the same substantive conclusions, which bounds the risk.","warn":""},
  {"id":109,"cat":"Statistical literacy","weight":1,"q":"Your anonymity hypothesis was directional. Did you test it one-tailed?","a":"No — two-tailed throughout. The reversal vindicates that choice: a one-tailed test in the predicted direction would have had zero power to detect the effect that actually exists. Directional theory paired with a non-directional test is the conservative combination, and here it is the only one that could have found the result.","warn":"This one is a gift if you answer it cleanly. Do not hesitate."},
  {"id":110,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":3,"q":"Your title foregrounds AI use and trust. The AI block contributed almost nothing. Shouldn't this thesis have a different title?","a":"The title names the research question, not the result. The thesis asked whether AI use, trust and personality add to classic predictors of cyber-aggression, and the answer for the AI block is no — a legitimate, reportable answer to exactly the question posed. Retitling after seeing the data to hide the question would be the results-driven move, the same error as dropping H1 after the anonymity reversal.","warn":"Same logic as the H1 defence: the question is honest even when the answer is null."},
  {"id":111,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":2,"q":"Moral disengagement predicting hostility — isn't that just common sense with confidence intervals?","a":"The direction is intuitive; the structure is not. Common sense does not predict that MD at β = .47 dwarfs every competitor, that anonymity runs negative against forty years of disinhibition theory, or that the AI block adds nothing. The contribution is the ranking and the anomalies, not the sign of one coefficient.","warn":"Never concede 'just common sense' — and never sneer at the question either. Answer it with the ranking."},
  {"id":112,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":2,"q":"You propose MIRM, and your own data prefer GAM on every fit criterion. Why should this thesis pass?","a":"Because the load-bearing claims are empirical — MD dominance and the AI-block null — and they stand independently of MIRM. MIRM is reported as an exploratory framework that loses on fit and is retained only for the dissociation signal, with both fragilities disclosed. Reporting adverse results against one's own framework is precisely the behaviour examination is meant to certify.","warn":"Do not defend MIRM's fit. Defend the honesty of reporting it."},
  {"id":113,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":2,"q":"You've spent decades building software and AI systems. Wasn't this thesis always going to find that AI matters?","a":"The data answered that: the AI block was null and I report it as null, in the headline. The venting trace is presented as fragile and its failure in the saturated model is disclosed. If professional investment biased the work, the bias lost. An adverse headline finding, published, is the strongest available evidence against motivated reasoning.","warn":"This question is a gift dressed as an attack. The null IS your alibi."},
  {"id":114,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":2,"q":"What is the weakest part of this thesis — and don't say the sample.","a":"The AI-construct instrumentation: first-generation, partly self-authored items with only face and content validity established. That is the same answer I give to every version of the weakness question, deliberately — it is the true weak point, and it is the one the follow-up programme is built to fix.","warn":"Your weakness answer must be identical every time it is probed (cf. the biggest-weakness question). Variance reads as evasion."},
  {"id":115,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":1,"q":"If we required corrections, what do you expect they would be?","a":"Most likely tightening residual causal language in the discussion and expanding the AI-construct validity limitations. I would accept both without argument; they align with limitations the thesis already names. Predicting your own corrections is the self-assessment the viva exists to test.","warn":""},
  {"id":116,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":2,"q":"How do we know you wrote this thesis and not your AI tools?","a":"Three lines. The AI-use log documents exactly what was delegated. The supervision constraint was that AI produced no dissertation prose. And the viva itself is the verification instrument: ask me about any analytic choice, any paragraph, any number, now, without notes. The examination is the authorship test, and I welcome it.","warn":"Calm, not defensive. Offer the live test before they do."},
  {"id":117,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":1,"q":"Convince me this isn't just another underpowered student survey.","a":"For the question that mattered, it was not: the classic block carried f² = .282, comfortably detected at n = 139. Where the design was underpowered — the tiny AI increments at f² ≤ .026 — that is stated, quantified, and routed to the follow-up programme. An underpowered block that says so, with effect sizes, is how instrument development is supposed to proceed.","warn":""},
  {"id":118,"cat":"Curveballs & challenge","weight":1,"q":"Name one published finding that contradicts yours, and answer it.","a":"The disinhibition lineage — for example Lapidot-Lefler and Barak's experimental work — links anonymity to increased toxic disinhibition, against my negative coefficient. My response is contextual: SIDE predicts anonymity amplifies the salient norm, and in an identified university population the salient norm is non-hostile. The contradiction is conditional on context, not absolute, and it is exactly what the discussion chapter addresses.","warn":"Have one contradiction pre-loaded. Improvising citations in the room is how errors happen."},
  {"id":119,"cat":"Viva process & opening","weight":3,"q":"Talk us through your thesis. You have five minutes.","a":"Fixed six-beat skeleton, one minute each: (1) problem — online hostility, and whether AI interaction belongs in its predictive models; (2) gap — MD tested mostly in adolescents, AI constructs never tested against classic predictors; (3) design — vignette survey, N = 164 valid, hierarchical regression onto HRL; (4) findings — MD dominates (β = .47), anonymity reverses, AI block null (largest ΔR² = .020), one fragile venting trace; (5) limits — cross-sectional, single-item DV, first-generation AI measures; (6) programme — the three-study escalation. Then stop.","warn":"Highest-leverage rehearsal available. Out loud, timed, until it lands at 5:00 ± 15 seconds."},
  {"id":120,"cat":"Viva process & opening","weight":2,"q":"Why this topic — and why you?","a":"Lead with the intellectual question: does AI interaction change the pathway from provocation to hostility? Then position, briefly: a career building software systems, now studying the psychology of their use. Two sentences, credentials as scaffolding for the question, then stop talking.","warn":"Do not inventory the career. The question is about the thesis."},
  {"id":121,"cat":"Viva process & opening","weight":1,"q":"Summarise each chapter in one sentence.","a":"Literature: establishes the MD and disinhibition lineage and the AI measurement gap. Methodology: defends the vignette design and the pre-data CAI-to-HRL switch. Results: the blocked models, the null, and the exploratory MIRM tests. Discussion: ranks the findings, explains the anomaly, routes the null to measurement. Prepare your own four sentences and rehearse them as a unit.","warn":""},
  {"id":122,"cat":"Viva process & opening","weight":1,"q":"What did you learn as a researcher — not what did you find?","a":"That measurement is the discipline: every hard lesson in this thesis was instrument-level — the CAI failure, the md2 contamination, the first-generation AI items. And that reporting against your own framework is harder, and worth more, than confirming it.","warn":""},
  {"id":123,"cat":"Viva process & opening","weight":2,"q":"Which question are you hoping we won't ask?","a":"Answer it by answering it: name the boundary-condition fragility — split-only, with an empirically weak impellor — or the md2 contamination, and give the full disclosure on the spot. The trap is hesitation; the escape is volunteering your sharpest known weakness fluently.","warn":"Never say 'none'. Never actually dodge. Pick the wound and show it is dressed."},
  {"id":124,"cat":"Viva process & opening","weight":1,"q":"Do you have any questions for us?","a":"One, prepared and forward-looking: their advice on taking the venting signal and the anonymity reversal to publication — which they would prioritise, and where. It signals the work continues past the credential and ends the viva on the programme, not the defence.","warn":""}
];
